// @error: because 'v' is being iterated
//
// `v.push(x)` inside the loop was rejected and `v[0] = x` was not — one rule, two
// answers depending on how the mutation was spelled. The assignment path exempts any
// index-qualified target because an in-place element write never reallocates, so a live
// slice VIEW stays valid and simply observes it. That reasoning does not extend to a
// loop: the loop is handing out that element, so rewriting it changes what the current
// binding names. Fixed by recording WHY a variable is frozen (view vs iteration) and
// applying the exemption only to views.
//
// Not a use-after-free — the slot does not move, so the loop reads the new value rather
// than freed memory. It is an iterator-invalidation semantics bug, and it is here
// because the inconsistency is what lets the next one through.
fn main() {
    var v: Vec<string> = Vec.new()
    v.push("a")
    v.push("b")
    for it in v {
        v[0] = "clobbered"
        print(it)
    }
}
